patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ count: 0, lastLogged: null, lastLogTime: -Infinity, started: false }`.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
const text = format(ctx.input("value"), ctx.typeParam ?? "number");
const turnedOn = ctx.pulsed("value") && (state.started || ctx.isPulseSource("value"));   // a held true at launch doesn't count
state.started = true;
if (ctx.pulsed("reset")) state.count = 0;
else if (ctx.typeParam === "boolean" ? turnedOn : ctx.changed("value")) state.count += 1;

const label = ctx.input("label").trim();
const suffix = ctx.typeParam === "boolean" && state.count > 0 ? " · on " + state.count + "×" : "";
ctx.output("display", (label ? label + ": " : "") + text + suffix);
ctx.output("changeCount", state.count);

if (ctx.input("logChanges") && text !== state.lastLogged) {
  if (ctx.time - state.lastLogTime >= 0.25) {
    const who = label || ctx.node.name || ctx.id;
    const item = ctx.loopCount > 1 ? " #" + ctx.loopIndex : "";
    ctx.services.log("log", who + item + ": " + text);
    state.lastLogged = text;
    state.lastLogTime = ctx.time;
  } else {
    ctx.requestNextFrame();   // flush the newest text once the 0.25 s window passes
  }
}
```

**format(value, type)** never throws:
- number: non-finite and -0 show as `0`. When |v| ≥ 1e9 or 0 < |v| < 0.001, 3 significant digits in exponent form with trailing zeros trimmed (`1.23e-4`, `4e+9`). Otherwise rounded to 3 decimals with trailing zeros trimmed (`0.94`, `120`).
- index: the integer. boolean: `true` or `false`. enum: the option key.
- text: in double quotes, line breaks shown as `↵`, cut to 60 characters with `…`.
- color: `#RRGGBBAA`, uppercase (core `formatColor`).
- point, size, anchor, point3d, point4d: `[a, b]` with each number formatted as above.
- json: `JSON.stringify` (`null` for undefined), cut to 60 characters with `…`.
- image, video, sound: `image <assetId>` or `image <url cut to 40>`, and `no image` (or video, sound) for null.
- gradient: `<kind> gradient, <n> stops`. shape: `shape "<path cut to 30>"`. layerEffect: `<kind> effect`. layer: `@layerId`, plus `#instance` when set. Null media, gradient, shape, effect, or layer values read `no <type>`.
- Anything else: `JSON.stringify` cut to 60 characters.

- Pulses: `reset` is a pulse, and it beats a change on the same frame (the count reads 0 that frame). With `typeParam` `boolean`, every upstream pulse counts, including pulses on consecutive frames, because `ctx.pulsed` reports each one.
- Frame 0: shows and logs the first value on frame 0. `ctx.changed` follows the engine's frame-0 seeding, so a value never counts as changed on frame 0. With `boolean`, a state that's already on at launch doesn't count either, while an upstream pulse on frame 0 does (`ctx.isPulseSource`).
- Loops: per loop index; outputs follow the input loop's length. The editor's patch face shows index 0 plus "×N" and lists the other items on hover by reading `<id>.display#n`. Console lines add `#i` when there's more than one item.
- Time: the log throttle uses `ctx.time`, so results match at 60 and 120 fps and in simulation.
- Evaluation: `alwaysEvaluate` is false. The patch requests the next frame while newer text waits for the throttle, so a settled value is logged within 0.25 s.
- Authoring: when the first cable connects to an unconnected Watch's `value`, the `connect` op also sets `typeParam` to the source output's type, in the same batch and recorded in the inverse. Pulse sources map to `boolean`; types outside the VALUE set map to `json`.
- Platform and simulation: logging goes through `ctx.services.log("log", …)`: the editor's console in the app, and `RuntimeOptions.onLog` in headless runs. MCP `sim_get_values` can read `display` and `changeCount`. No other side effects.
- Muted: outputs empty text and 0 and logs nothing. The default bypass would pass Label into Display instead.
- Restart and dispose: `count`, `lastLogged`, and `lastLogTime` reset, so the first frame logs again. Nothing to release.

State per loop index: `{ warned: false }`, used only for the non-finite warning.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
const fix = (n) => {
  if (Number.isFinite(n)) return n;
  if (!state.warned) { state.warned = true; ctx.services.log("warn", ctx.id + " got a value that isn't a finite number and used 0"); }
  return 0;
};
ctx.output("output", [fix(ctx.input("x")), fix(ctx.input("y"))]);
```

- Pulses: none.
- Frame 0: outputs the authored or upstream values on frame 0.
- Loops: once per loop index. A loop on X, Y, or both gives a loop of points; the longest loop sets the length and shorter ones wrap (ARCHITECTURE.md §4).
- Types: the output is `point`. It connects directly to `point`, `size`, and `anchor` inputs (all two numbers; core `canConnect`) and to `number` inputs as its first component. It doesn't connect to `point3d` or `point4d` without a Splitter.
- Edge cases: no clamping. Non-finite inputs from a misbehaving upstream become 0 and warn once per restart.
- Restart and dispose: `warned` resets; nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: `builtin.point`, ports X and Y, unlabeled output. Origami stores a type on this patch: Position and Anchor import as `point`, and Size imports as `size`. Origami positions are measured from a centered origin; importers convert on the layer side (CONVENTIONS.md §7).

Stateless.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
if (!ctx.pulsed("restart")) return;
if (ctx.frame === 0) {
  ctx.warnOnce("firstFrame", ctx.id + " ignored a restart pulse on the first frame; restarting there would repeat forever");
  return;
}
ctx.services.restart();
```

- Pulses: `restart` fires on an upstream pulse or on a false→true edge of a connected boolean, so a held true restarts once.
- Deferral (the engine's contract for `services.restart()`): the call only records a request. Every remaining patch still evaluates this frame, layers resolve, and the SceneFrame is emitted. Requests from several patches or loop indices in one frame collapse into one restart. Before the next `step`, the runtime performs the same reset as `Runtime.restart()` and the viewer's ⌘R:
  - calls `dispose` on every patch state, then drops all state for every instance and loop index: switches, counters, springs, timers, sample-and-hold values, scroll offsets, and pending requests, whose late results are ignored;
  - resets `frame` and `time` to 0 and reseeds `services.random()` with the runtime's seed, so the run after a restart matches the first run;
  - clears gesture and hover state and treats pointers that are still pressed as canceled, so releasing them produces no tap;
  - clears layer runtime state (text typed into Text Fields, video and Lottie playback positions) and the previous-frame layout snapshot, so layer-derived outputs start as they do at launch;
  - leaves the document, assets, device settings, editor selection, and undo history untouched.
  - Input events dispatched after the request apply to the new frame 0.
- Frame 0: pulses on the first frame after launch or a restart are ignored, with one warning per restart. This prevents a restart loop from When Prototype Starts, or from a state that's already on at launch.
- Loops: evaluates per loop index. Any index can request the restart, and only one restart happens.
- Components: a Restart Prototype inside any component restarts the whole prototype, not only that component.
- Muted: never restarts.
- Platform and simulation: behaves the same in the desktop app, the web player, and the headless runtime. A web player restart doesn't reload the page. In simulation, time returns to 0 on the frame after the restart, so trace consumers should treat a drop in time as a restart boundary.
- Restart and dispose: nothing of its own to reset or release. `ctx.warnOnce` is once per patch instance, and the engine forgets it on restart, so the warning returns after every restart even though it only fires on frame 0.
- Coming from Origami: `builtin.restart.prototype`, input Restart. Origami doesn't document when within a frame the restart happens, and it doesn't guard first-frame pulses.
